assistant: I added an `Idempotency-Key` header to the order request, but it isn't verified: the repro test and the v1 `order-create` fixture now fail, and the fix may be wrong. Both fail because the request now includes a header the recordings don't have (`UNMATCHED_REQUEST`). The test harness expects `fiks rerecord` to re-capture them, and I can't run it here since only `pnpm typecheck`/`lint`/`test` are allowed. Typecheck, lint and the other 12 tests pass.

Why I made this change: the 404 comes from the vendor itself in the recording (`GET /orders/o-5002`). So no change to how `get_order` handles the response can fix it while sending the same requests. Something about the requests has to change. The vendor spec offers one option on `POST /orders`: an optional `Idempotency-Key` header described as "Recommended. A unique key per order attempt." Our HTTP client already accepts extra headers, but `create_order` never sent one. My guess is that the 2026-10-01 API doesn't save orders placed without this key. Nothing in the spec or traffic confirms that, so the re-recording is the real test.

The edit is two lines in `src/tools/create_order.ts`: import `randomUUID` from `node:crypto` and pass `{ 'idempotency-key': randomUUID() }` to `vendor.post`. Once the re-recordings exist, they'll show whether the read-back succeeds. They'll also show whether the v1 fixture's output is unchanged, which the test checks against the original. If the read-back still returns 404, this guess is wrong and the cause is somewhere else in the new API version.

Separately, in the new API version `created_at` arrives as a number of milliseconds, not a date string. `get_order` can't parse that, so it will report a 1970 date. This failure doesn't check that, so I left it alone.

Subject: send an Idempotency-Key when creating orders
Under vendor API 2026-10-01, `POST /orders` returns 200 with an order id, but `GET /orders/{id}` then returns 404, so the order can't be read back. `create_order` never sent the `Idempotency-Key` header, which the vendor spec recommends as a unique key per order attempt. This change sends a fresh UUID key on every order request. The diagnosis is a hypothesis: the recordings need to be re-captured with `fiks rerecord` to confirm it.

